WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.119
A Pod can hold more than one container.

2
00:00:05.119 --> 00:00:07.228
So when it starts, who goes first?

3
00:00:07.228 --> 00:00:10.144
And what happens if one of them never finishes?

4
00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:13.359
Before we answer, picture a waiter with a tray.

5
00:00:13.359 --> 00:00:19.852
The tray may hold a main dish and a side, but they always travel together, and land on the same table.

6
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:21.424
A Pod is that tray.

7
00:00:21.424 --> 00:00:26.352
One or more containers, which Kubernetes places together on one node.

8
00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:28.711
They share three things.

9
00:00:28.711 --> 00:00:32.185
One IP address, so they reach each other on localhost.

10
00:00:32.185 --> 00:00:35.852
Folders called volumes, that they can all read and write.

11
00:00:35.852 --> 00:00:40.548
And one life: they start on the same node, and they are removed together.

12
00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:42.804
Most Pods hold just one container.

13
00:00:42.804 --> 00:00:48.737
But the tray matters for helpers that only work right next to the app, because they need its files.

14
00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:52.293
Here is the example Pod from the lab.

15
00:00:52.293 --> 00:00:54.494
Its app is nginx, a web server.

16
00:00:54.494 --> 00:00:58.967
It serves one web page, and writes every visit into a log file.

17
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.574
It needs two helpers.

18
00:01:00.574 --> 00:01:05.416
One writes the page into a shared folder called work, before nginx starts.

19
00:01:05.416 --> 00:01:09.865
The other reads each new line of the log, from a folder called logs.

20
00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:16.082
Now the trouble with the old way, where a helper was just a second, ordinary container.

21
00:01:16.082 --> 00:01:18.571
Each dot is one log line from nginx.

22
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.065
A log shipper that started late missed the first lines.

23
00:01:22.065 --> 00:01:24.557
One that stopped early lost the last ones.

24
00:01:24.557 --> 00:01:29.721
Like a runner who shows up after the first plates went cold, and leaves before dessert.

25
00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:33.494
A real kitchen has two kinds of helper for this.

26
00:01:33.494 --> 00:01:39.229
Before the cook starts, a prep cook chops the onions and lays out the plates, then goes home.

27
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:45.051
A runner arrives early, stays all evening, and leaves only after the last dish is out.

28
00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:46.676
Kubernetes has both.

29
00:01:46.676 --> 00:01:49.142
The prep cook is an init container.

30
00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:50.833
The runner is a sidecar.

31
00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:53.905
First the rule for init containers.

32
00:01:53.905 --> 00:01:56.501
They run first, one at a time, in order.

33
00:01:56.501 --> 00:02:01.823
Each must exit with code 0, which means all went well, before the next one starts.

34
00:02:01.823 --> 00:02:04.289
Only then do the app containers start.

35
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.923
Now look at the manifest.

36
00:02:06.923 --> 00:02:10.769
Under initContainers: write-page, then log-tailer.

37
00:02:10.769 --> 00:02:12.615
Under containers: nginx.

38
00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:15.818
But a log reader follows the log forever.

39
00:02:15.818 --> 00:02:16.909
It never exits.

40
00:02:16.909 --> 00:02:20.985
By the rule we just learned, nginx waits for it to exit.

41
00:02:20.985 --> 00:02:22.951
So does nginx wait forever?

42
00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:28.084
If the rule were the whole story, yes.

43
00:02:28.084 --> 00:02:30.564
Our dish would never leave the kitchen.

44
00:02:30.564 --> 00:02:32.727
But log-tailer has one extra line.

45
00:02:33.433 --> 00:02:35.020
restartPolicy: Always.

46
00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.057
For an init container, that one line makes it a sidecar.

47
00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:41.998
For a sidecar, Kubernetes does four things.

48
00:02:41.998 --> 00:02:44.730
It starts it in init order, before the app.

49
00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.113
It moves on as soon as it is running, without waiting for it to exit.

50
00:02:49.113 --> 00:02:50.955
It restarts it if it crashes.

51
00:02:50.955 --> 00:02:54.703
And it stops it only after the app containers have stopped.

52
00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:59.279
Let us watch it start, the way the lesson does.

53
00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:02.594
At first nothing runs, and STATUS says Init:0/2.

54
00:03:02.594 --> 00:03:05.632
The first of two init containers is running.

55
00:03:05.833 --> 00:03:11.870
write-page writes index.html into the work folder, and exits with code 0.

56
00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.855
The prep cook goes home.

57
00:03:13.855 --> 00:03:15.923
STATUS moves to Init:1/2.

58
00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:17.867
Next, the sidecar starts.

59
00:03:17.867 --> 00:03:24.317
As soon as it is running, it counts as ready, one of two, while nginx is still being created.

60
00:03:24.533 --> 00:03:25.808
Then nginx starts.

61
00:03:25.808 --> 00:03:30.269
READY becomes two of two, because READY counts the sidecar too.

62
00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:32.322
The prep cook is not counted.

63
00:03:32.322 --> 00:03:33.243
He went home.

64
00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:38.231
Now ask nginx for its page, from inside the Pod, on localhost.

65
00:03:38.433 --> 00:03:40.262
hello from the init container.

66
00:03:40.262 --> 00:03:44.042
The prep cook has gone home, but the onions are still chopped.

67
00:03:44.042 --> 00:03:50.443
The page lives in the work folder, and the folder belongs to the Pod, not to the container that wrote it.

68
00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:55.089
Ask for a page that does not exist too, then read the sidecar's logs.

69
00:03:55.089 --> 00:03:59.832
nginx wrote both visits into the logs folder, and the runner printed them.

70
00:04:00.533 --> 00:04:03.005
And the old problem with the log lines?

71
00:04:03.005 --> 00:04:07.822
The sidecar starts before the app, and stops only after the app has stopped.

72
00:04:07.822 --> 00:04:09.343
So every line gets read.

73
00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:13.046
So should everything go on one tray?

74
00:04:13.046 --> 00:04:13.252
No.

75
00:04:13.252 --> 00:04:17.797
Share a Pod only when you must share a node, an address, or files.

76
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.400
An app and its database belong in separate Pods, so you can scale and replace each one on its own.

77
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.163
A tray, not a moving truck.

78
00:04:26.967 --> 00:04:28.413
The moral of the story.

79
00:04:28.413 --> 00:04:31.810
Prep cooks first, one at a time, and each must finish.

80
00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:34.390
The runner next, and it stays to the end.

81
00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:35.270
Then the dish.

82
00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:38.541
And all of them share the Pod's address and folders.

83
00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:39.866
One quick check.

84
00:04:39.866 --> 00:04:44.471
A Pod like ours shows READY one of two, and STATUS PodInitializing.

85
00:04:44.471 --> 00:04:46.189
Who is running right now?

86
00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:49.732
The sidecar.

87
00:04:49.732 --> 00:04:54.311
write-page has already finished, and nginx is still being created.
